<article class="env-lemma" id="02IP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02IP">Lemma <span data-tag="02IP">28.8.2</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is Cohen-Macaulay, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is locally Noetherian and all of its local rings are Cohen-Macaulay, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is locally Noetherian and for any closed point $x \in X$ the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is Cohen-Macaulay. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00NB" data-tag="00NB">10.104.5</a> says that the localization of a Cohen-Macaulay local ring is Cohen-Macaulay. The lemma follows by combining this with Lemma <a href="/tag/01OW" data-tag="01OW">28.5.2</a>, with the existence of closed points on locally Noetherian schemes (Lemma <a href="/tag/02IL" data-tag="02IL">28.5.9</a>), and the definitions. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>